<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Menelaus</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">In Greek mythology, Menelaus was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>, the brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agamemnon">Agamemnon</a> and husband of Helen. When Helen left with the Trojan prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, he and Agamemnon led the Greek expedition against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troy">Troy</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odyssey">Odyssey</a> he is shown back in Sparta, reconciled with Helen, and he was honoured with her at the Menelaion near Sparta.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/menelaus.jpg" alt="Menelaus" width="320" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Giacomo Brogi · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brogi,_Giacomo_(1822-1881)_-_n._4140_-_Roma_-_Vaticano_-_Menelao_-_Busto_in_marmo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Menelaus was a descendant of Pelops, son of Tantalus, and the younger brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agamemnon">Agamemnon</a>. In the usual version, followed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>'s Iliad and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odyssey">Odyssey</a>, the two were sons of Atreus, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mycenae">Mycenae</a>, and Aerope. Later sources say that Atreus feuded with his brother Thyestes over the throne of Mycenae, and that Thyestes took it after his son Aegisthus murdered Atreus. Menelaus and Agamemnon went into exile and returned with the help of King Tyndareus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> to drive Thyestes away. Agamemnon took the throne of Mycenae.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>When Tyndareus's stepdaughter Helen came to be married, many kings and princes sought her hand. On the advice of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odysseus">Odysseus</a>, all the suitors swore to defend the chosen husband in any quarrel, and straws were drawn. Menelaus won and became ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> with Helen. A supposed palace has been found at Pellana in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laconia">Laconia</a>, though other archaeologists think it too far from other Mycenaean centres to have been his capital.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, the Trojan prince, ran off to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troy">Troy</a> with Helen. Invoking the oath, Menelaus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agamemnon">Agamemnon</a> raised a fleet of a thousand ships. In Book 3 of the Iliad Menelaus beats Paris in a duel, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aphrodite">Aphrodite</a> carries Paris away. In Book 4 Pandarus wounds Menelaus with an arrow, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a> protects him from death, and in Book 17 he recovers the corpse of Patroclus. According to Hyginus he was one of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> hidden in the Trojan Horse, and at the sack he killed Deiphobus. Ancient versions tell how he raised his sword against Helen and then spared her.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>According to Book 4 of the Odyssey Menelaus's fleet was blown by storms to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, where he forced Proteus to reveal the way home, and he reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> eight years after leaving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troy">Troy</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>'s account Helen never went to Troy but spent the war in Egypt. In the Odyssey the couple live in harmony at Sparta, though some scholars detect underlying conflict.</p><p>Menelaus and Helen were worshipped at the Menelaion in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/therapne">Therapne</a>, and only Pausanias says they were buried there. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odyssey">Odyssey</a> it was ordained that Menelaus would not die but go to Elysium, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euripides">Euripides</a>'s Helen he spends his afterlife on the Isle of the Blessed. He appears in Greek vase painting from the 6th to the 4th century BCE and in 5th-century tragedies by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sophocles">Sophocles</a> and Euripides.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Menelaus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The region had been important in the Mycenaean age: a palace at Ayios Vasileios and a mansion at the Menelaion near the later city date from the Late Bronze Age, and Homer made Lacedaemon the kingdom of <b>Menelaus</b> and Helen.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sparta">Sparta · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Guided by the goddess Athena, his son Telemachus travels to Nestor and to <b>Menelaus</b> in Sparta for news.</blockquote><a href="/work/odyssey">Odyssey · What It Is</a></li><li><blockquote>Homer remembered Laconia as the kingdom of <b>Menelaus</b> and Helen.</blockquote><a href="/state/mycenaean-kingdom-of-laconia">Mycenaean Kingdom of Laconia</a></li></ul></section>
